<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <AssemblyName>NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime</RootNamespace>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>

    <!-- NuGet package (co-versioned with NumSharp: the version is the repo-root Directory.Build.props
         VersionPrefix, never a number in this file, so the nuspec dependency on NumSharp and this
         package's own version cannot drift apart). -->
    <PackageId>NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime</PackageId>
    <!-- UNRELEASED: merged, but hidden until the owner releases it. With IsPackable=false, `dotnet pack`
         is a silent no-op (exit 0, no .nupkg), so CI packs, signs, publishes and lists nothing for this
         project, and ../../docs/website-src/docfx.json excludes its docs page. To release it, set this
         back to true and publish the page again: "Unreleased packages" in ../../.claude/rules/interop-packages.md. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <Authors>Eli Belash, Haiping Chen, Meinrad Recheis</Authors>
    <Product>NumSharp</Product>
    <Company>SciSharp STACK</Company>
    <Copyright>2021 © SciSharp STACK Team</Copyright>
    <Description>Zero-copy interop between NumSharp NDArray and ONNX Runtime (Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime) tensors. Feed an NDArray to InferenceSession.Run as an OrtValue over the very same unmanaged buffer (no per-pixel fill loop, no copy), read outputs back as NDArrays (copy or zero-copy view), and post-process with NumSharp (softmax, argmax, top-k, clip) instead of hand-written loops. Conversion library only: no engine backend, no ModuleInitializer, no native dependency of its own — bring the ONNX Runtime native package (CPU, Gpu, DirectML, ...) you already use.</Description>
    <PackageTags>NumSharp;NumPy;ONNX;onnxruntime;OrtValue;DenseTensor;inference;machine-learning;interop;zero-copy;scientific-computing</PackageTags>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://scisharp.github.io/NumSharp/docs/interop/onnxruntime.html</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/SciSharp/NumSharp</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>
    <!-- No PackageReadmeFile, deliberately: the package embeds no README, so each version's README
         tab stays owner-editable on nuget.org, where the release notes are pasted after publishing.
         README.md in this folder is repo documentation only and is not packed. See "No embedded
         package README" in ../../Directory.Build.props, whose guard fails `dotnet pack` if this returns. -->
    <!-- Packed explicitly (`dotnet pack`), never on build: see the release workflow. License
         acceptance and the icon are repo-wide settings in ../../Directory.Build.props. -->
    <PackageOutputPath>../../packages</PackageOutputPath>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- Symbols: embed the .pdb in the .nupkg (matches NumSharp.Core / NumSharp.Interop.pythonnet). The
         release pipeline uploads/pushes only *.nupkg, so a separate .snupkg would be dropped. -->
    <AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder>$(AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder);.pdb</AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- In-repo: ProjectReference (packs as a NuGet dependency on "NumSharp" $(Version)). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../NumSharp.Core/NumSharp.Core.csproj" />

    <!-- The ONLY external dependency: the MANAGED half of ONNX Runtime — the C# API (OrtValue,
         InferenceSession, DenseTensor, TensorElementType, Float16). Deliberately NOT the native
         `Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime` package: ORT ships its native runtime as MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE flavours
         (`Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime` = CPU, `.Gpu` = CUDA/TensorRT, `.DirectML`, `.QNN`, ...; two of them
         side by side is a documented misconfiguration), and every one of them depends on this same
         `.Managed` package. Depending on `.Managed` alone composes with WHICHEVER flavour the consumer
         already references — the consumer must reference exactly one, which they always do to run
         inference at all. The tests reference the CPU flavour.

         The range is BOTH knobs at once (the same lesson as NumSharp.Interop.pythonnet's pythonnet pin):

         (a) FLOOR = what we compile against: the OLDEST release exposing every member this package
             uses, 1.16.0 (Sept 2023) — the release that "expose[d] OrtValue API as the new preferred API
             to run inference in C#" and made Float16/BFloat16 full-featured. Every member used here
             (CreateTensorValueWithData, CreateAllocatedTensorValue, CreateTensorWithEmptyStrings,
             StringTensorSetElementAt, GetStringTensorAsArray, GetTensorMutableRawData,
             GetTensorTypeAndShape, GetTensorMemoryInfo, the OrtValue Run overloads incl. pre-allocated
             outputs, RunAsync, NodeMetadata.ElementDataType / AsOptionalMetadata,
             OverridableInitializerMetadata) is present there: the test suite runs against 1.16.0.
             ONLY THIS NUGET RANGE ENFORCES THE FLOOR. This comment used to say that the runtime refuses
             an older ORT with a FileLoadException, because the managed assembly "bumps its
             AssemblyVersion every release". It does not at the floor: the 1.16.0 assembly carries
             AssemblyVersion 0.0.0.0 (1.29.0 carries 1.29.0.0, 1.30.0 1.30.0.0, 1.31.0 1.31.0.0; read
             from the packages), so the reference this package is compiled with binds to ANY ONNX
             Runtime assembly. An ORT below the floor that got past NuGet would fail later, with
             MissingMethodException at the first member it lacks.
             One feature has a higher floor, checked at run time: RunIntoAsync / RunAsync read the loaded
             assembly's version and refuse one older than 1.30.0, the first whose managed RunAsync pins
             the arrays the native run keeps reading (0.0.0.0 counts as older).
         (b) FLOOR = what a bare consumer installs. NuGet resolves LOWEST-applicable, never latest — but a
             consumer's native flavour reference (e.g. Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime 1.31.0) carries its own
             `.Managed` dependency at the same version, so NuGet unifies upward to THAT. In practice the
             consumer's ORT version wins; this floor only stops an ancient one from sneaking in.

         (c) NO UPPER BOUND, on purpose (the owner's decision of 2026-11-03, after F52 opened the ML.NET
             bridge's range the same way). A cap below the consumer's ONNX Runtime is not a guard but a restore
             ERROR - NuGet resolves cousin ranges that do not overlap as NU1107 - and since F16 the ML.NET bridge
             depends on this package, so the former [1.16.0, 2.0.0) would have failed the restore of ONNX
             Runtime AND ML.NET bridge users the day they moved to an ONNX Runtime 2.0. ORT's own packages
             declare this dependency open too (Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime 1.31.1 -> .Managed version="1.31.1";
             Microsoft.ML.OnnxTransformer 5.0.1 -> .Managed version="1.23.2"). The bridge uses ORT's public C#
             API plus the private _disposed flags OrtDisposalProbe reads (a missing field turns its check off,
             never the call), so a breaking ORT major would show at run time, not at restore - which is why
             CI's advisory "ONNX Runtime: Test newest (net10.0, advisory)" step runs the suite on the newest
             ONNX Runtime on nuget.org, release candidates included, to see it first.

         Verified against 1.16.0 (the floor) and 1.31.1 (current) by the test project's OnnxRuntimeVersion
         matrix, and against the newest release by the advisory step. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Managed" Version="1.16.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
